1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,040
Let's start our new analysis right away.

2
00:00:03,040 --> 00:00:08,660
Today we will explore a rigorous economic study published in the prestigious journal PNAS

3
00:00:08,660 --> 00:00:12,160
by researchers Niko Voidglender and Anzio Akinvott.

4
00:00:12,160 --> 00:00:13,320
The goal?

5
00:00:13,320 --> 00:00:16,199
To explore a truly profound and complex topic.

6
00:00:16,199 --> 00:00:20,400
Can beliefs and attitudes be modified on a large scale

7
00:00:20,400 --> 00:00:25,280
and, above all, can historical indoctrination leave a measurable imprint

8
00:00:25,280 --> 00:00:28,480
in the minds of people decades later?

9
00:00:28,640 --> 00:00:33,200
In short, diving into this data will reveal hidden social dynamics,

10
00:00:33,200 --> 00:00:38,640
of how past policies inexorably continue to shape our present.

11
00:00:38,640 --> 00:00:43,560
The big question driving this research is as simple as it is mysterious.

12
00:00:43,560 --> 00:00:48,120
Can extreme prejudices and beliefs really be inculcated to the point of enduring

13
00:00:48,120 --> 00:00:49,840
for over half a century?

14
00:00:49,840 --> 00:00:54,040
To solve this puzzle, the study sets aside guesswork and relies on the science

15
00:00:54,040 --> 00:00:55,439
of pure and raw data.

16
00:00:55,479 --> 00:00:59,719
Indeed, it cross-references modern opinion surveys with highly detailed historical records.

17
00:00:59,719 --> 00:01:04,439
It is a rigorous methodological approach, perfect for understanding cultural persistence over time

18
00:01:04,439 --> 00:01:08,799
and the actual effectiveness of a system aimed at modifying the mind of an entire generation.

19
00:01:08,799 --> 00:01:13,719
And this brings us straight to the contemporary data, the true starting point of the investigation.

20
00:01:13,719 --> 00:01:18,120
The researchers examined responses from an important German general social survey,

21
00:01:18,120 --> 00:01:24,239
ALLBUS, focusing in particular on data collected in 1996 and 2006.

22
00:01:24,239 --> 00:01:30,759
We are talking about a huge sample, over 5,000 respondents spread across 264 different cities.

23
00:01:30,759 --> 00:01:36,560
An absolutely fundamental pool of information to objectively measure what the population thinks,

24
00:01:36,560 --> 00:01:40,039
many years after the end of the regime of the 1930s and '40s.

25
00:01:40,039 --> 00:01:43,879
Now, modern data offers a truly thought-provoking picture.

26
00:01:43,879 --> 00:01:50,239
They highlight that a significant portion of today's population still holds negative views today.

27
00:01:50,240 --> 00:01:54,240
When faced with specific questions, the answers were revealing.

28
00:01:54,240 --> 00:02:02,840
For example, 25.7% of respondents said they would feel uncomfortable with the idea of an intermarriage within their own family.

29
00:02:02,840 --> 00:02:09,360
And not only that, 21.5% even stated that there should not be equal rights.

30
00:02:09,360 --> 00:02:14,840
But there is one number in particular worth dwelling on: 25%.

31
00:02:14,840 --> 00:02:20,759
A quarter of the analyzed population still harbored mildly to strongly negative opinions.

32
00:02:20,759 --> 00:02:24,759
The researchers define this as a broad measure of prejudice.

33
00:02:24,759 --> 00:02:26,199
That is an incredible figure, isn't it?

34
00:02:26,199 --> 00:02:28,280
And it deepens the mystery enormously.

35
00:02:28,280 --> 00:02:34,039
Especially when we consider that this data was collected 50 years after the fall of this power system.

36
00:02:34,039 --> 00:02:37,080
Where does this deep-rooted dispersion of opinions come from?

37
00:02:37,080 --> 00:02:42,080
Economic analysis suggests we look back to the period when those people were formed.

38
00:02:42,160 --> 00:02:45,960
To find the root of the problem, let's take a leap back in time,

39
00:02:45,960 --> 00:02:49,640
shining the spotlight directly on the impact of the 1930s.

40
00:02:49,640 --> 00:02:55,960
The study's focus is on those specific demographic cohorts born during that era.

41
00:02:55,960 --> 00:03:04,400
We are talking about people who lived their formative years under a state apparatus that made racial indoctrination its absolute pillar.

42
00:03:04,400 --> 00:03:11,400
The basic theory is clear: those who grew up in that decade were exposed to extreme ideological conditioning.

43
00:03:11,439 --> 00:03:16,879
At an age when, as we know, the mind is extremely susceptible to external influences.

44
00:03:16,879 --> 00:03:20,879
Looking at this graph extracted directly from the original document,

45
00:03:20,879 --> 00:03:23,960
the result of that exposure immediately catches the eye.

46
00:03:23,960 --> 00:03:26,120
There is an impressive anomaly in the data.

47
00:03:26,120 --> 00:03:31,640
The cohort born in the 1930s has nearly 10% committed extremists.

48
00:03:31,640 --> 00:03:38,240
This share is two to three times higher than generations born after 1950.

49
00:03:38,360 --> 00:03:44,240
The researchers point out that even when accounting for factors such as education level or economic situation,

50
00:03:44,240 --> 00:03:49,159
those born in those years are exactly twice as likely to hold extreme views.

51
00:03:49,159 --> 00:03:53,080
But behind the statistics, of course, there are human lives.

52
00:03:53,080 --> 00:03:56,840
Historical memoirs help us put a face to these concepts.

53
00:03:56,840 --> 00:04:03,120
Consider this excerpt from the book by Alfons Heck, who as a young boy was part of the youth organizations of the time.

54
00:04:03,120 --> 00:04:09,879
He writes: "we swallowed our daily dose of nationalist instruction as naturally as our morning milk."

55
00:04:09,879 --> 00:04:13,439
Indoctrination began very early, at 5 or 6 years old.

56
00:04:13,439 --> 00:04:20,680
Just consider that the official youth training manual dedicated 45 of its 105 pages solely to racial ideology.

57
00:04:20,680 --> 00:04:24,360
It was a pervasive and inescapable daily practice.

58
00:04:24,360 --> 00:04:29,480
At this point we might ask ourselves which medium was more impactful.

59
00:04:29,600 --> 00:04:33,480
Comparing the various forms of propaganda at the time, a clear gap emerges.

60
00:04:33,480 --> 00:04:39,280
On one hand, there were radio and cinema, considered state-of-the-art, and on the other, traditional methods,

61
00:04:39,280 --> 00:04:42,280
namely compulsory education and youth groups.

62
00:04:42,280 --> 00:04:46,720
Well, by cross-referencing data on radio subscribers and cinema tickets per capita,

63
00:04:46,720 --> 00:04:51,720
the study shows that local access to media, incredibly, had poor predictive power.

64
00:04:51,720 --> 00:04:59,439
On the contrary, it was schools and youth organizations that proved extraordinarily effective in permanently shaping the mindset of a generation.

65
00:04:59,920 --> 00:05:07,000
And here we come to the final, perhaps most surprising piece of this investigation: the weight of pre-existing prejudices.

66
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:12,279
Economists have indeed discovered the secret ingredient that greatly amplified this effect.

67
00:05:12,279 --> 00:05:18,439
In practice, they wondered why regime education worked perfectly in some cities,

68
00:05:18,439 --> 00:05:21,720
while in others the impact was much more limited.

69
00:05:21,720 --> 00:05:28,279
The answer lies not only in the message transmitted, but in the historical soil in which those seeds were sown.

70
00:05:28,400 --> 00:05:37,360
To prove this theory, the investigation digs even deeper into the past, analyzing historical voting records long before the rise of the regime.

71
00:05:37,360 --> 00:05:43,840
We are talking about years in which, as early as after 1870, discriminatory petitions began to circulate,

72
00:05:43,840 --> 00:05:48,640
such as the one presented to Bismarck, which gathered as many as 265,000 signatures.

73
00:05:48,640 --> 00:05:57,120
Then, between 1890 and 1912, elections began to appear where radical parties gained strong support in specific areas,

74
00:05:57,120 --> 00:06:00,920
in some districts exceeding 40% of the vote.

75
00:06:00,920 --> 00:06:05,879
And look what happens when we correlate the votes from the late 19th century with the surveys of the 1990s,

76
00:06:05,879 --> 00:06:08,000
a clear correlation emerges.

77
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:12,399
The level of intolerance in the contemporary world is enormously higher in those areas that,

78
00:06:12,399 --> 00:06:16,759
historically, had supported discriminatory parties over a century earlier.

79
00:06:16,759 --> 00:06:21,000
In localities that fell into the top third for radical electoral support,

80
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:23,639
modern extremists are close to 8%.

81
00:06:23,639 --> 00:06:29,680
This represents a fourfold increase compared to areas where that historical support was just over 2%.

82
00:06:29,680 --> 00:06:34,919
The authors of the paper analyze this phenomenon, defining it as the amplification effect.

83
00:06:34,919 --> 00:06:39,719
In short, institutions and schools acted as a giant multiplier,

84
00:06:39,719 --> 00:06:44,399
precisely where prejudice was already present in families and communities.

85
00:06:44,399 --> 00:06:46,519
From an economic and psychological point of view,

86
00:06:46,519 --> 00:06:49,879
this is unequivocal proof of the power of confirmation bias.

87
00:06:49,879 --> 00:06:54,399
Propaganda does not act in a vacuum; it clings to and leverages pre-existing beliefs,

88
00:06:54,399 --> 00:06:59,240
triggering a lasting cultural transmission passed down from generation to generation.

89
00:06:59,240 --> 00:07:05,360
To sum up, the research confirms that those old school policies have by no means vanished into thin air,

90
00:07:05,360 --> 00:07:08,879
but have generated a true echo in our modern society.

91
00:07:08,879 --> 00:07:13,680
An echo that we can mathematically measure even in generations born decades later,

92
00:07:13,680 --> 00:07:17,560
such as in 1965 or 1975.

93
00:07:17,560 --> 00:07:22,040
Those children of the 1930s, once grown, formed families and communities,

94
00:07:22,040 --> 00:07:26,839
transmitting in turn prejudices powerfully amplified by confirmation bias.

95
00:07:26,839 --> 00:07:30,519
And this closes the circle of our explanation,

96
00:07:30,519 --> 00:07:35,519
leaving us, however, with a very profound reflection drawn from the emerging data.

97
00:07:35,519 --> 00:07:42,360
In light of these numbers, how vulnerable is society today to the cultural transmission of beliefs?

98
00:07:42,360 --> 00:07:45,319
The long shadow of history and the power of education

99
00:07:45,319 --> 00:07:49,639
show us that deeply rooted ideas in the social fabric require decades,

100
00:07:49,639 --> 00:07:52,639
if not centuries, to be understood and deconstructed.

101
00:07:52,639 --> 00:07:54,680
Beliefs are inherited, it is true,

102
00:07:54,680 --> 00:07:59,879
but an objective awareness of the data can be the first great step toward breaking this chain.